/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:459:      "notes": "Section 21 CDCC adult/disabled-dependent care inputs (PolicyEngine/populace#451 item 1), previously structural zeros in both the retired eCPS and every Populace base. Source decision receipts (Census API variable dictionaries, ASEC 2024 and SIPP 2023, read 2026-07-22): ASEC carries the qualifying concept directly - person item PEDISDRS ('difficulty dressing or bathing') operationalizes 'physically or mentally incapable of self-care', with SIPP's identical ESELFCARE item corroborating; NEITHER instrument's current public-use release carries in-household adult-care expenditures: ASEC's only care-expense items are childcare HCHCARE_VAL/SPM_CHILDCAREXPNS, and while the 2018 SIPP panel's Child & Dependent Care topical module collected paid dependent-care fields (ECREPAYANYON, TDEPNDNTEXP), they are absent from the 2023 SIPP PUF dictionary (the pinned donor vintage), whose only adult-care payment amount TDPCAREAMT covers care of a former household member, failing section 21's same-principal-abode requirement. The dollar leg is therefore an explicitly documented same-instrument proxy: the measured ASEC work-related childcare expense distribution (the same section 21 employment-related expense class, subject to the same 26 USC 21(c) per-person cap) supplies the paid-care usage rate and the positive level distribution (weighted quantiles, zero-weight donors excluded). The donor universe is measured at SPM-unit grain, where the childcare leaf lives: units with any member under the 21(b)(1)(A) age limit and any positive member earnings, on the measured ASEC channel - an approximation of the binding childcare population at donor grain, not a reproduction of the tax-unit minimum-earnings test. Recipient support is restricted to tax units where the statute can bind: a measured disabled qualifying individual under 21(b)(1)(B) (dependent) or 21(b)(1)(C) (married head or spouse), plus the 21(d) earnings structure computed exactly as the engine's min_head_spouse_earned binds - both spouses earning, or a 21(d)(2) floor-eligible spouse (incapable of self-care, or the measured full-time college student via is_full_time_college_student) deemed while the OTHER spouse actually earns; deeming never rescues a unit whose only earner is the floor-eligible spouse. Selection is a seeded weight-targeted permutation prefix over sorted unit ids (invariant to person-row order) at the measured usage rate; levels are a distribution-preserving weighted-quantile draw whose grid is the selected units' own cumulative-weight midpoints, placed on the qualifying person with the smallest person_id. PolicyEngine-US 1.764.6 owns all downstream CDCC formulas; is_incapable_of_self_care is also read by SNAP/Medicaid work-requirement exemptions and state CDCC analogs, which move those programs from flag-off defaults toward the measured disability structure."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1717:      "notes": "Direct mappings of measured ASEC hours variables, identical to the retired enhanced-CPS pipeline: weekly_hours_worked_before_lsr <- HRSWK (usual hours per week last year, 0 for non-workers), hours_worked_last_week <- A_HRS1, weeks_worked <- WKSWORK clipped to [0, 52]. Nothing is imputed. Without this stage PolicyEngine-US defaults every person to 40 weekly hours, so the SNAP 30-hour general and 20-hour ABAWD work requirements pass universally (populace #242/#248). HRSWK is an annual usual-hours measure while the ABAWD standard is a monthly 80-hour test; week-by-week volatility is not observable in the CPS and is deliberately out of scope."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1729:          "locator": "Census CPS ASEC SPM unit reported SNAP subsidy (SPM_SNAPSUB)"
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1735:          "locator": "USDA FNS SNAP participation-rate estimates (Reaching Those in Need)"
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1755:      "notes": "Reported recipients (SPM_SNAPSUB > 0) always take up \u2014 survey measurement first. Non-reporting units receive seeded draws at exactly the rate needed for the overall weighted take-up share to land on the cited FNS participation rate; when reporters alone exceed the rate the share is emergent. The flag is assigned across all SPM units and the engine intersects it with modeled eligibility, matching the retired enhanced-CPS pipeline. Draws are keyed by stable source identity so support-channel clones of one source unit always agree. Without this stage the engine defaults every unit to take-up (100% participation), overstating the reach of eligibility-side reforms (populace #243). The national fill is an assignment prior: the snap_state_take_up stage recalibrates it per state to FNS caseloads (populace #372)."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1767:          "locator": "Census CPS ASEC SPM unit reported SNAP subsidy (SPM_SNAPSUB)"
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1773:          "locator": "USDA FNS SNAP average monthly households by state (usda_snap.fy2024.state_average_monthly_households.*)"
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1794:            "usda_snap_households_by_state"
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1806:      "notes": "Anchored count-calibration to FNS state caseloads (populace #372), following the medicaid_take_up pattern at SPM-unit grain. Reported recipients always take up; the fill draws at an in-build state rate (FNS average-monthly household count over weighted modeled-eligible units, an assignment prior, never cited as provenance) and is greedily calibrated to the FNS FY2024 state counts among eligible non-anchored units. Caseload semantics are fiscal-year average-monthly stocks - the same rows the snap_households weight-calibration targets compile from, so the seed and the calibration objective agree. CPS receipt underreporting is strongly state-dependent, so the national snap_take_up fill leaves the worst-underreporting states short of taker households no reweighting can recover. The assign operation deliberately carries no eligibility mask: off-domain units keep a draw-based propensity so eligibility-expanding reforms do not inherit a hard-coded zero response. States whose FNS count meets or exceeds modeled eligible weight saturate (all eligible units take up) and are recorded as saturated in diagnostics, not failed. Persons caseloads are deliberately untargeted: the SNAP assistance unit is often a subset of the SPM unit, so member counts overcount FNS participants by roughly half."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:1979:      "notes": "Direct mappings of measured ASEC demographic variables reproducing the retired enhanced-CPS pipeline's constructions (each cited to the retired pipeline's source files in the stage module): is_disabled <- any PEDIS* difficulty item == 1, aligned with reported SSI (under-65 SSI_VAL > 0 implies disabled \u2014 the retired align_reported_ssi_disability rule from datasets/cps/takeup.py, applied to is_disabled in cps.py); is_blind <- PEDISEYE == 1; is_full_time_college_student <- A_HSCOL == 2 & A_FTPT == 1 (the retired primary branch; census_cps.py loads A_FTPT, so the published surface ran the strict form \u2014 the A_HSCOL-only fallback for loads without A_FTPT is not reproduced); own_children_in_household <- count of household members whose PEPAR1/PEPAR2 parent pointers resolve to the person within PH_SEQ; veterans_benefits <- VET_VAL. Nothing is imputed. Without this stage every input takes its engine default (False/0), erasing SNAP disability, student, parent/child, and veteran exemption channels (populace #244/#248). is_veteran is formula-owned in PolicyEngine-US (derived from veterans_benefits) and is deliberately not exported (populace #24)."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:2017:      "notes": "The CPS ASEC does not ask about pregnancy, so is_pregnant is seeded rather than mapped, matching the retired enhanced-CPS pipeline: women aged 15-44 receive stable blake2b draws against the pregnancy rate (point-in-time rate = annual births x 39/52 over the female 15-44 population; 4.1% nationally, the retired pipeline's national rate). The retired pipeline varied the rate by state using live CDC VSRR + Census ACS fetches; populace builds are hermetic, so the national rate applies uniformly and state-level variation is follow-up work under populace #351. Without this stage is_pregnant defaults to False for everyone and the SNAP ABAWD pregnancy exemption (7 U.S.C. 2015(o)(3)(E)) never fires."
/Users/maxghenis/PolicyEngine/populace/packages/populace-build/src/populace/build/us/source_stages.json:2109:      "notes": "State agencies may exempt a monthly-average share of ABAWD-covered individuals from the SNAP time limit (7 U.S.C. 2015(o)(6)): 8% from FY2024 (15% FY1998-2019, 12% FY2020-23) \u2014 the cap PolicyEngine-US carries as gov.usda.snap.work_requirements.abawd.discretionary_exemption_rate. Coverage is engine logic the build must not duplicate, so the flag is seeded with stable blake2b draws across the potentially covered population (adults 18-64) and the engine intersects it with modeled coverage; draws being independent of coverage keeps the exempt share among covered individuals at the statutory rate in expectation. Seeding at the cap assumes full state usage, which overstates actual practice: USDA reporting shows states typically use well under the allotment (exemption usage exceeded the cap in only five state-year instances across FY2016-19), so this is an upper bound on the discretionary channel; refining toward USDA exemption-usage reports is follow-up under populace #323. Without this stage the input defaults to False and the discretionary channel never fires (populace #323)."

